ntrols key central squares, and prepares kingside castling.\n\nReason: Ng8-f6 is a highly principled developing move. It brings a knight into the game, controls important central squares (e4 and d5), prepares for kingside castling, and does not weaken my position. It's one of the most common and effective responses in many openings (like the Sicilian or Pirc when responding to an early Nc3). It avoids passive pawn moves and prioritizes rapid, active development.\n\nAnswer: N: g8-f6"}
