ported.\n- R: h8-g8 – Prepares kingside castling via rook lift, but premature without king safety or central control established.\n- N: b8-c6 – Develops knight toward center, supports d4/e5 breaks later.\n- N: b8-a6 – Less ideal; retreats knight to edge, possibly preparing c5 or b4 pressure, but slow.\n\nBest options appear to be P: d7-d5 (immediate central control) or N: b8-c6 (development). Among these, d7-d5 stands out as it immediately seizes space and activates pieces.\n\nReason: Playing P: d7-d5 fights for the center, opens lines for the queen and bishop, and follows classical opening principles. It increases my control over key squares and accelerates development. This move often leads to dynamic counterplay, which is essential when facing early White activity.\n\nAnswer: P: d7-d5"}
