o e5, f6, g7, h8 — yes! h8 is on that diagonal. e8? From d4 to e8: that’s d4-e5-f6-g7-h8 — not aligned. d4 is light-squared, e8 is dark — no.\n- White knight on f1 (NWF1): can it reach e8? f1 → e3, d2, etc. — no jump to e8.\n- White rooks: one on b5 (RWF1) — attacks b-file and 5th rank. e8 is not on b-file or 5th rank.\n- Another rook on h5 (RWF1) — h-file and 5th rank. h8 is on h-file — so rook on h5 controls h8, h7, h6, h5… so **h8 is attacked by white rook on h5**.\n\nAh! **h8 is attacked** — and for kingside castling, the king must not pass through or end on attacked squares. Since g8 and h8 are both in the castling path (O-O), and **h8 is attacked by rook on h5**, castling kingside is illegal.\n\nSimilarly, queenside: king moves to c8, passing through d8 — are d8 or c8 attacked?\n- d8: is it attacked? Rook on b5? No. Rook on h5? No. Bishop on d4? d4 to d8? Yes — same file? d4 is d4, d8 is d8 — yes, vertical. So **d4 bishop attacks d8**.\n\nSo **d8 is attacked by white bishop on d4** — therefore, king cannot castle queenside either (must pass through d8).\n\nHence, king moves are limited to non-castling — explains why only K:e8-d8, e7, f7, f8 are listed.\n\nBack to evaluation:\n\nThe standout move is **N: c4-a5**, because:\n- It attacks the white rook on b5\n- The rook on b5 (RWF1) is **undefended** — no white piece on a5, c5, b4, b6, etc.\n- After Na5, white loses the rook unless they move it — but even then, I win time or material.\n\nIs a5 safe for the knight? Can it be captured?\n- After Nc4-a5, knight on a5 — can white capture it?\n    - Pawn on a4? No — black pawn on a4? No — white has pawn on a4? Looking: no — board shows no pawn on a4. White has pawn on c3 (PWF1), but not on a4.\n    - White knight on f1 — too far.\n    - White bishop on d4 — can it go to b6? b6 is not on d4’s diagonal (d4 is light-squared; b6 is dark). No.\n    - White pawns: none near.\n    - So **knight on a5 is safe after capture**.\n\nTherefore, **N: c4-a5 wins a full rook for free*