\n1 |NWF1|KWF1|    |BWF1|    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a7-a5, P: a7-a6, P: b7-b6, R: b8-a8, R: b8-c8, R: b8-d8, R: b8-e8, N: c4-a3, N: c4-a5, N: c4-b2, N: c4-b6, N: c4-d2, N: c4-d6, N: c4-e3, N: c4-e5, B: e6-d5, B: e6-d7, B: e6-c8, B: e6-f7, P: f5-f4, R: f8-e8, R: f8-d8, R: f8-c8, R: f8-f7, R: f8-f6, K: g8-h8]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Situation: I am playing as Black, and currently have a somewhat passive position. My king is on g8, still in the corner, and has not moved (KBF1), meaning castling kingside might be a possibility if it were legal — but the rook on h8 has already moved (RBF1), so kingside castling is no longer viable. The rook on f8 is still unmoved and connected with the king-side structure. White has a knight on c3 and bishop on d4, both potentially active, and pawns advancing on the queenside (a4, c3, e3). My pieces are partially developed: the knight on c4 is active and attacking key central squares, the dark-squared bishop on e6 exerts pressure along the diagonal, and two rooks are on open files.\n\nWhite just played a4 (implied by PWF1 on a4), which may support b5 or restrict my b7 pawn. My pawn on a7 is still on its starting square (PBF0), so it can move one or two steps forward.\n\nEvaluation:\n- **P: a7-a5**: Pushes the a-pawn, challenging White’s a4 pawn and preventing b4 expansion. It also opens a potential retreat square for the rook on a8 later. However, it doesn’t contribute directly to development or attack.\n- **P: a7-a6**: A quieter move, supporting b5 or preparing …b6, but loses tempo compared to a5.\n- **N: c4-b6** or **N: c4-d6**: Both reposition the knight, possibly to support e4 or counter central play, but they retreat from an active post. c4 is a strong outpost, so retreating should be avoided unless tactical.\n- **N: c4-e5**: Jumps toward the center, eyeing f7 or d7 pressure. But f7 is defended, and this might trade off favorably for White.\n- **B: e6-f7**: Retreats the bishop passively; not ideal.\n- **B: e6-c8**: Retreats all the way to c8, extremely passive.\n- **B: e6-d7** or **B: e6-d5**: d5 is a strong central square, challenging White’s control. Bd5 would pin a knight on c3 or pressure e4. This seems promising.\n- **R: f8-e8**: Activates the rook, possibly preparing doubling on the e-file or supporting e5. But e5 isn’t under direct threat yet.\n- **R: b8-a8**: Connects rooks and prepares potential action on the a-file, especially after …a5.\n- **P: f5-f4**: Advances the f-pawn, gaining space and possibly preparing …f4-f3 to undermine White’s center. But it weakens e4 and may overextend.\n\nBest options:\n- **B: e6-d5** stands out — it develops the bishop to a strong central square, pressures White’s position (possibly threatening to exchange on c4 or attack e4), and improves piece activity.\n- **N: c4-e5** is also active, but less flexible — e5 may become a target.\n- **R: f8-e8** is solid, but less urgent than developing the bishop.\n\nReason: The bishop on e6 is slightly passive. Moving it to d5 increases pressure on the center, challenges White’s development, and may force concessions. It’s a tempo-gaining developing move that enhances coordination.\n\nAnswer: B: e6-d5'}
