om the board: row 8, h column is blank), so h8 is a legal destination.\n\nThus, re-evaluating:\n- g8-g7: lands on a square occupied by **White\'s rook** → **illegal**\n- Only remaining move: g8-h8\n\nBut is h8 safe? h8 is empty, and not currently attacked. The White pieces on d1 (BWF1), a1 (NWF1), c3 (PWF1), d4 (no), etc., do not attack h8 directly. No White queen or bishop on a diagonal to h8. The White bishop on d1 is light-squared; h8 is dark, but it doesn’t reach it. Knight on a1 is far away. So h8 is safe.\n\nAlso, since the king has already moved (KBF1 — Moved=1), castling is not possible anyway.\n\nTherefore, despite initial listing, **g8-g7 is invalid due to occupancy**, so the only legal move is **g8-h8**.\n\nBut the system says "your valid moves are" — implying they are pre-filtered. So likely, the environment already filtered only legal moves. But it listed both g8-g7 and g8-h8.\n\nWait — contradiction: if g7 has RWF1 (White rook), then g7 is occupied by opponent — can we capture?\n\nYes! If g7 has a White rook (enemy), then **g8-g7 would be a capture**, which should be allowed — unless there are other restrictions.\n\nSo why is g8-g7 listed as a valid move? Only if it\'s legal.\n\nBut let’s confirm: at g7: RWF1 — White rook. So Black king can capture it via g8-g7.\n\nIs that safe?\n\nLet’s see: after Kxg7, would the king be in check?\n\nCheck potential attackers:\n\n- White bishop on d1 (BWF1): attacks diagonals. From d1 to g4? d1-e2-f3-g4-h5 — no, doesn\'t hit g7. g7 is on f6/g7/h8 diagonal — from d1, the diagonal is a4-d1, then e2, f3, g4, h5 — so no, BWF1 does not attack g7.\n\n- White knight on a1 (NWF1): a1-b3-c5-d7? d7 is not near g7. a1-c2-e3-f5 or g4 — no path to g7 in one move. So no knight check.\n\n- White pawn on f3 (PWF1)? f3 to g4 — no, pawns capture diagonally forward — from f3, could capture on g4 — but g7 is way above. So no.\n\n- Any other White piece? Rook on g7 — but that\'s the one being captured.\n\nSo after Kxg7, king is not in check.\n\nMoreover, the king started on g8 (has moved), so moving it is fine.\n\nSo g8-g7 is a legal **capture** of the White rook.\n\nNow compare:\n\n- g8-g7: captures a White rook — material gain: rook is valuable (5 points). King becomes more centralized.\n\n- g8-h8: retreats to corner, avoids capture, but loses opportunity to win material.\n\nTherefore, **g8-g7 is clearly superior** — it captures a rook with no immediate danger.\n\nBut why is RWF1 on g7? That seems like a blunder by White — leaving a rook en prise.\n\nUnless... is there a pin or discovered attack?\n\nSuppose Black plays Kxg7 — is there a discovered check?\n\nThe only pieces behind the king are on the back rank. But the king is moving from g8 to g7 — along the g-file.\n\nIs there a White piece on the g-file that could give discovered check? No — the rook on g7 is White, but it\'s being captured. Behind it? g6 is empty, g5 has PBF1 (Black pawn), so no.\n\nAlternatively, diagonal? No discovered check possible because the king isn\'t blocking any diagonal or file.\n\nSo no tactical refutation.\n\nTherefore, capturing the rook is safe and strongly advantageous.\n\nFinal evaluation:\n- K: g8-g7 — wins a rook, improves king position, safe from check — excellent\n- K: g8-h8 — passive, allows White to recover or reposition, loses initiative — inferior\n\nThus, best move is g8-g7.\n\nAnswer: K: g8-g7'}
