ved.\n\nSo RWF1: White Rook, has moved (so it's now on b7 after moving — likely just played Rb7).\n\nSo White just played Rb7 — possibly offering a sacrifice? Or blundered?\n\nBut regardless, it's undefended. So we can capture it safely.\n\nNow, between R: b8-b7 and B: f3-b7 — both win material.\n\nBut: if we play B: f3-b7, we expose the back rank? Not really — king is on g7, and b7 is far.\n\nBut more importantly: what about the knight moves?\n\nN: e5-f7 — attacks nothing immediately, but f7 is near the center. Could support e5-d5 or e5-g6 later.\n\nBut less urgent than capturing a rook.\n\nK: g7-f6/g6/h6/h8/f8 — king movement: risky. Why move the king into the center when you can gain material?\n\nUnless there's a threat — but there is no immediate check.\n\nSo the best options are the rook or bishop capture on b7.\n\nNow, which one?\n\nIf we use the rook (b8-b7), we place a rook on b7 — could later threaten b2, etc., but White’s king is on a1 — very odd.\n\nWait — White king is on a1 (KWF1) — has moved, so probably not castled. Possibly in danger.\n\nBut we have no immediate attack.\n\nStill, doubling rooks or increasing pressure on the b-file might be good.\n\nBut: using the bishop to capture on b7 might be better because:\n\n- The rook on b8 is already positioned to control the b-file; removing it weakens file pressure.\n- The bishop is somewhat undeveloped, and this activates it.\n- But: after B: f3-b7, the bishop may become trapped? b7 is deep in enemy territory.\n\nFor example, after ...Bb7, White plays Ra1-b1, winning the bishop.\n\nBecause White has a rook on a1 — if it moves to b1, it attacks the bishop on b7.\n\nAnd the bishop has no escape — only back to f3, but that loses tempo.\n\nWhereas if the rook takes on b7, it's a rook for a rook — equal material, and rooks belong on open files anyway.\n\nBut: wait — is it a rook for rook? Yes — White’s rook on b7 was just moved there — so we capture it with either piece.\n\nBut if we capture with the bishop, we