5-g4 opens the possibility of advancing the pawn and opening lines near White’s center, but may not be urgent.\n- R: f8-e8 or other rook moves prepare central control, but less impactful than b7-b1.\n- K: g7-g8 or K: g7-f7 improves king safety slightly, but not critical yet.\n\nThe most forcing and promising move is R: b7-b1, attacking the undefended white king directly. Since there is no piece defending b1, and the white king has no escape squares nearby, this creates an immediate threat of check or even checkmate depending on response.\n\nReason: R: b7-b1 delivers maximum pressure by targeting the enemy king directly along the open file. It forces White to respond (likely with a rook or queen interposition if available, or king move), gaining tempo and potentially winning material. Even if White blocks, Black gains initiative.\n\nAnswer: R: b7-b1"}
