sted.\n\nBut a8 is far, not helpful.\n\nOr B: f3-b7 — b7 is near a7 — my pawn on a7.\n\nBut not useful.\n\nWhat about **rook on f8**? It can come into play — f8-f3? Is that possible? f8 to f3 — yes, R: f8-f3 is not listed? Wait, valid moves include: R: f8-e8, f8-d8, ..., f8-f7, f8-f6, ..., f8-f4, f8-f5 — yes, f8-f4 is listed, f8-f5, etc. But f8-f3? Not listed — probably blocked by bishop on f3? f3 has BBF1 — my bishop — so yes, bishop on f3 blocks the file.\n\nSo rook cannot go to f3 or below.\n\nBut if I move the bishop first, then rook can penetrate.\n\nFor example, **B: f3-b7** — removes bishop and opens the f-file.\n\nThen next move, rook f8-f3 or f8-f2, attacking the seventh rank and possibly targeting the White king.\n\nBut White king is on a3 — not on seventh.\n\nMore importantly, after B:f3-b7, the diagonal is cleared, but not immediately helpful.\n\nBut b7 is occupied? No — b7 is empty — yes.\n\nBut b7 is near my pawn on a7 — could support promotion.\n\nBut not urgent.\n\nBack to immediate tactics.\n\nConsider **knight on e5 to d3** — N: e5-d3.\n\nd3 is close to the center. From d3, it attacks b2, b4, c1, c5, e1, e5, f2, f4.\n\nDoes it attack a3? d3 to a3: 3 left, 0 down — no. 2 down + 1 left? d3-c1 or e1 — not a3. So no.\n\nBut d3 is a strong outpost.\n\nHowever, **N: e5-c4** is better because it attacks b2 and a3 — and b2 is an escape square.\n\nIn fact, after N:c4, b2 is attacked, cutting off one escape.\n\nEarlier I thought b2 is attacked — yes.\n\nBut in the case of rook check on a1 or b3, king can go to b2 — but if knight is on c4, b2 is attacked, so king cannot go there.\n\nSo **combining knight on c4 with rook check** could be powerful.\n\nBut I can only make one move.\n\nSo best is to **play N: e5-c4**, attacking a3 (check), and removing b2 as an escape.\n\nEven though king can still go to a2, a4, b3, b4, it’s a strong threat.\n\nBut wait — is **N: e5-c4** even giving check? Only if c4 attacks a3.\n\nc4 to a3: difference is 2 files left (c→a), 1 ra